  <p class="answer-label">Answers</p>
  <h1 class="font-display">A binge eating app without food tracking</h1>

  <p class="answer-lead">
    Most binge eating and eating disorder apps are built on logging — food diaries, calorie
    counts, weigh-ins, streaks. If tracking makes things worse for you, you're not imagining it:
    research consistently shows that calorie counting and food tracking can worsen binge eating
    for many people. Kippi is a binge eating support app with no tracking of any kind.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Why tracking backfires for binge eating.</strong> Logging every bite keeps your
    attention on food all day — and for many people that hypervigilance <em>is</em> the noise.
    The numbers become one more thing to be behind on, one more scorecard for shame. To be clear,
    the problem is the scorekeeping — not eating regularly. Kippi's answer isn't a stricter set
    of rules; it's no rules to track at all. And shame is fuel for the next urge, not protection
    from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Why "after the fact" isn't enough.</strong> Most apps meet you at the reflection
    stage: what did you eat, how did you feel, fill in the form. But the moment that decides
    everything is 20 minutes earlier, when the urge is rising and nothing else exists. Support
    that only arrives afterward misses the moment you actually needed someone.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What to look for instead.</strong> People we've listened to describe the same short
    list: something that's there <em>during</em> the urge, not after. Something with no numbers
    anywhere. And something that doesn't punish you for slipping — because a tool you dread
    opening after a hard night is a tool you'll stop opening at all.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display">Common questions</h2>

  <h3>Is there a binge eating app that doesn't track food?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Kippi does not ask about food, calories, portions, weight, or body size — ever. There is
    no logging, no tracking, and no numbers of any kind. It's an AI companion for the urge moment
    itself: you open it and talk, and it stays with you while the feeling rises and falls.
  </p>

  <h3>Why do food tracking apps make binge eating worse for some people?</h3>
  <p>
    Research consistently shows that calorie counting and food tracking can worsen binge eating
    for many people. Constant tracking keeps attention locked on food and turns every day into a
    scorecard — and the shame of a "bad" number tends to feed the cycle rather than interrupt it.
  </p>

  <h3>Does Kippi have streaks?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Streaks that reset when you slip are clinically backwards for binge eating recovery —
    relapse is a normal and expected part of the recovery arc, not a failure. Kippi stays open.
    Nothing resets.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display">What Kippi is — and isn't</h2>

